  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a relatively small sample of 13 US cities, which may not capture the full diversity of curb management practices in smaller municipalities or international contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzes Commercial Vehicle Load Zone (CVLZ) programs across 13 US cities, evaluating their policy, regulatory, and infrastructure planning frameworks. The comparative analysis reveals that most of these cities allocate less than one percent of their total curb space to freight, with denser networks clustered in Seattle and San Francisco. The research establishes a formal typology of how cities classify load zones, define eligible users, allocate access, and monetize these spaces.",
      "why": "It provides the first formal definition and systematic typology of urban freight curb management programs, establishing a baseline measure of how little physical curb space is actually dedicated to commercial deliveries in major US cities."
    }
